 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Yes. Investors pay a higher advertised standard variable rate, and the gap has been steady at about 0.58 percentage points since early 2022.\n\n**Standard variable, advertised (RBA table F5):**\n- **Owner-occupier:** 8.77% p.a. in September 2026\n- **Investor:** 9.35% p.a. in September 2026\n- **Gap:** 0.58 percentage points\n\nThe gap was the same in January 2022 (4.52% vs 5.10%) and has stayed at 0.58 points through every rate move since, including the 2023 peak and the 2025–26 cuts and rises.\n\n**For comparison, discounted variable:**\n- **Owner-occupier:** 6.80%\n- **Investor:** 7.13%\n- **Gap:** 0.33 percentage points\n\nSo the investor premium is smaller on discounted loans than on the standard product.\n\nThese are advertised rates. The RBA notes that rates actually paid on new loans are lower, and its table F6 has those figures if you want the effective rates.",
